provides the mathematical grounding for analyses based on computational complex-
ity, a subject left to future research. Thirdly, the method allows the introduction of
control features, again representable in terms of abstract algebra, which model some
of the control features of a typical business enterprise and can be used in evaluating
the strengths and weaknesses of those features vis-a-vis the firm which the structure
represents. The control features provided by the structure are not all encompassing,
but they would disallow a number of improper transactions from entering the system.
The system presented below is a representation of the conceptual core of an account-
ing systems. Any realistic implementation of it would of course require administrative
and behavioral control systems as well.

      In the point of view adopted here, the state of an accounting system at any instant
is described by a column vector whose entries are integers: positive, negative or zero.
The i th entry of this balance vector is the balance of the ith account. Here it is con-
venient to represent a debit balance by a positive integer and a credit balance by a
negative integer since this forces most typical asset balances to be positive and most
liability and equity accounts to be negative. It is also convenient to make all balances
integers, so amounts are considered as being either in cents or in whole dollars. Since
the system is double-entry bookkeeping, the sum of the entries in the balance vector
will always be zero, reflecting the fact that the system of accounts must always be in
balance.
      A basic concept used throughout this analysis is a transaction. This is a function
that operates on the existing balance vector to produce a new balance vector, by add-
ing a positive or negative number to each account involved in the transaction. It is
natural to represent a transaction as a vector whose entries sum to zero and which is
of the same dimension as the balance vector. A transaction is then thought of as the
vector addition of the transaction vector to the balance vector. In many cases, a trans-
action will only involve an exchange of funds between two accounts, in which event,
the corresponding transaction vector will have just two non-zero entries, one being
the negative of the other. These will be called simple transactions. More complex
transactions, however, will occur as, for example, in the sale of an asset where there
will be more than one positive andyor negative entry.
      In a real-life system, there would be certain transactions which would be
regarded as improper: for example, credit pension fund and debit equity account.
There should therefore be a list of allowable transactions, those that can actually be
