applied to the system. However, even if a transaction is allowable, it might still be
rejected if it caused an unacceptable balance in one of the accounts. For example,
customer accounts are likely to have credit limits. Thus, the algebraic structure
recognizes the existence of both allowable transactions and allowable balances. It
models for these types of integrity issues.
      A system of the type described can be conveniently visualized as an automaton
which checks each incoming transaction to see if it is allowable; if so, it computes the
new balance vector and checks to see if this too is allowable. If this is the case, it
records the new balance. The states of the automaton are thus the allowable balance
vectors of the system. The input alphabet consists of all transaction vectors (allowable
or not) and the transition function is a transition from one allowable balance vector to
another allowable balance function. Note that the transition functions are not quite
the same as the transaction functions since a transaction may map an allowable balance
vector to one which is not allowed, in which event the corresponding transaction
function would cause no change in the balance vector. The specific implementation
of balances and transactions is provided in section 2.
      There is a standard procedure which associates with an automaton an algebraic
structure called a monoid. A monoid is a set M with a distinguished element e, the
identity, and a rule for combining elements of M. This rule should satisfy the associa-
tive law and combining an element with e through this rule should leave the element
unchanged. For this reason, the structure of an accounting system described here gives
rise to a monoid. It is not always the case that the inverse of an allowable transaction
is allowable. In accounting systems where this is the case, however, the correspond-
ing monoid will have the additional property that each element x in M has an inverse.
A monoid with this property is called a group. These issues are addressed at length in
section 3.
      Section 4 begins the discussion of how to produce useful information from the
system. In order to produce a report on some aspect of an accounting system, one can
partition the accounts into equivalence classes. Accounts belonging to the same subset
are regarded as equivalent and are combined to form a single account. The balance of
the new account is the sum of the balances of the equivalent accounts. The same
procedure can be applied to the transaction vectors. The effect of such a procedure is
to produce a new accounting system known as a quotient of the original system. The
balance vectors of the original system would then feature in a report on the status of
the various sets of accounts that have been combined. The process of forming a
quotient structure from a given structure and an equivalence relation is a familiar
phenomenon in abstract algebra.
      The simplest instance of a quotient system is when the set of accounts is parti-
tioned into asset accounts, liability accounts, and equity accounts. The resulting quo-
tient system has three accounts: total assets, total liabilities, and total equity. There is
a natural function from the accounting system to any quotient of the system. This
function is an important example of a homomorphism between accounting systems.
