In sections 5 and 6, the general notion of a homomorphism will be shown below to
give an efficient way of classifying changes in accounting systems.
      In section 7, the problem of balance verification is addressed. What is required
here is an effective procedure to test if a purported final balance vector could actually
have arisen in an accounting system by legitimate operations. Accordingly, one must
decide if there is a finite sequence of allowable transactions which, when applied to
the original balance vector, will lead to the final balance vector. The sequence should
be such that all of the intermediate balance vectors are allowable. The existence of
such a verification procedure clearly enhances the auditibility of the system.
      Section 8 shows that for an important class of accounting systems, including
most that arise in practice, such an effective procedure does exist. For a given final
balance vector, the mathematical problem is to find out if it could be the solution to
a system of linear equations with integral coefficients. This is a problem of integer
programming and there are well-established algorithms to solve it. It will be shown
that these finitely specified accounting systems, which are the closest to real-world
accounting systems, are so formulated as to permit computer implementation in an
appropriate language.

