3.   The definition of an accounting system

      After the discussion of the preliminary notions of balance and transaction, it is
now time to give a precise definition of an abstract accounting system. Let n be a
positive integer and let A = {a1 , a2 ,…, an } be an ordered set of n distinct elements,
called the accounts. An accounting system on the set A is a triple A = (A|T|B), where
T and B are subsets of Bal(n) called, respectively, the sets of allowable transactions
and allowable balances. (Recall Bal(n) and Trans(n) are isomorphic.)
      The mode of operation of the system A can be conveniently visualized by means
of an automaton, Â . The input set, state set, and output set of this automaton are
Bal(n), B and B < {∗}, repectively, where the symbol ∗ stands for ERROR. The
transition function δ : B × Bal(n) → B and the next output function λ : B × Bal(n)
→ B < {∗} are given by

