                     δ ( b, υ ) = 
                                  b           otherwise;
                                                                                       (1)
                                  b+υ        if υ ∈ T and b + υ ∈ B,
                     λ ( b, υ ) = 
                                  ∗          otherwise.

      To see how the automaton operates, assume that it is in state b, so b is the present
balance vector. A transaction υ is fed into the system and read by the automaton. If
υ ∈T, i.e., υ is an allowable transaction, then the vector b + υ is computed. If the result
is an element of B, i.e, the balance vector b + υ is allowable, then b + υ becomes the
next state, the new balance vector. The new balance is then printed on the output tape.
If either condition is not met, the automaton remains in state b and prints ERROR on
the output tape. Notice that the input set of an automaton is usually taken to be a
finite set. However, this is not a problem in the present context since the vectors of
integers can be represented by a finite string of 0’s and 1’s with separation symbols.

