4.   Sub-accounting and quotient-accounting systems

      In the study of algebraic structures there are certain concepts which occur at an
early stage. One of these is the notion of a sub-structure, which is, roughly speaking,
a structure contained as a subset in a larger structure of the same type. Subgroups and
submonoids are examples of substructures.
      Less obvious is the notion of a quotient structure. The idea of a quotient structure
is to combine or identify certain elements of a structure in such a way as to produce
a new structure. This arises in accounting systems when the balances of certain
accounts are combined as, for example, in the process of defining a report.

4.1. Sub-accounting systems
     Consider two accounting systems A 1 = (A1|T1|B1 ) and A 2 = (A2|T2|B2), where
A1 # A2 and the order of accounts in A1 must be consistent with the order in A2 in that
the accounts in A2 which also appear in A1 must occur in the same relative order as in
A1. Suppose that if zero entries are inserted in vectors of T1 and B1 for accounts in A2
but not in A1, the resulting vectors are in T2 and B2 , respectively. Then A 1 is called
a sub-accounting system of A 2 . Thus, any transaction or balance vector allowed in
A 1 would correspond to an allowable vector in A 2 . For example, any accounting
system on a set of accounts A is a sub-accounting system of the universal accounting
system on A.

4.2. Quotient accounting systems
