A *. Two accounting systems A and A * are isomorphic if there exists an isomorphism
from A to A *. The idea here is that isomorphic accounting systems operate in ex-
actly the same manner, even though they may have a quite different set of accounts.

5.1. Examples of homomorphisms
      Next, a number of examples will be furnished which are designed to show how
the abstract notion of a homomorphism underlies several natural operations applica-
ble to accounting systems.

(i)  Changing the order of the accounts.
     The accounts in an accounting system A = (A|T|B) are written in some definite
order a1 , a2 ,…, an . Consider a change in the ordering; this amounts to forming a per-
mutation Π of the set {1, 2,…, n} and taking the new order to be a Π(1), a Π(2) ,…, aΠ(n) .
One can also regard Π as a function from A to A in which ai → a Π(i) . Let T * and B*
denote the arising sets of vectors when the vectors of T and B have their entries
permuted according to Π. For example, a typical vector in T * would be of the form υ *,
where υ ∈T and the ith entry of υ * is υ Π –1(i) . Then A* = (A|T *|B* ) is a new account-
ing system and Π : A → A* induces an isomorphism Π : A → A*. There is nothing
mysterious occurring here, merely a change in the order of accounts, balance entries
and transaction entries.

(ii) Addition of accounts, transactions and balances.
      Another natural operation that may be applied to an accounting system is the
addition of further accounts, allowable transactions and allowable balances. Suppose
that A is a sub-accounting system of a system A *. Thus, A = (A|T|B) and A * =
(A*|T *|B* ), where A # A*. Now the set inclusion function i : A → A* induces an
injective function i : Bal(n) → Bal(n* ). Moreover, it follows from the definition of a
sub-accounting system that i(T) # T * and i(B) # B*. Thus, i : A → A * is a mono-
morphism which we will call the inclusion monomorphism from A to A *. One can
regard A * as the system obtained from A by adding extra accounts, transactions and
balances. This procedure therefore results in a monomorphism from the original
system to the augmented system.
(iii) Combining accounts.
      The process of combining various sets of accounts in a system A by forming a
quotient system A yE was described above. It will be shown that there is a canonical
homomorphism from A to A yE which is surjective. This is a phenomenon which
occurs frequently in algebra: given a quotient structure, there is a surjective
homomorphism onto it from the original structure. What is important here is the
observation that the production of a report on an accounting system is yet another
example of a homomorphism.
