6.   The Homomorphism Theorem
      There is a well-known theorem in algebra asserting that, when a homomorphism
is given from a structure S to another structure S * of the same type, a certain quotient
structure of S is isomorphic with a sub-structure of S *. Theorems of this type hold for
monoids, groups, rings and many other algebraic structures. It will be shown below that
accounting systems too display such a theorem, which is called the Homomorphism
Theorem for accounting systems. But before this can be stated, it is necessary to
introduce a further concept.
      Let Θ : A → A* be a homomorphism of accounting systems induced by a func-
tion Θ : A → A *. Let Θ(A) denote the set of distinct accounts Θ(ai ) in the ordering
of A*, thus Θ(A) # A*. The surjective function from A to Θ(A) sending ai to Θ(ai ),
denoted by Θ*, induces Θ* : Bal(n) → Bal(n ′ ), where |Θ(A)| = n′. Thus, Θ*(T) #
Bal(n ′ ), Θ*(B) # Bal(n′ ). Define the image of Θ to be the accounting system
