Proposition. Let A = (A|T0|T1|B) be an accounting system with finite transaction
base. Then the set of allowable transactions is recursive. If in addition A is bounded,
then it is a recursive system.

Proof. Let T1 consist of all transactions of types t1 , t2 ,…, tk and let T = T0 < T1 be
the set of allowable transactions. If υ is any balance vector, there is a recursive
procedure to determine if υ ∈T. In the first place, one can check to see if υ ∈T0 since
T0 is finite. Secondly, one can compute the type of υ and determine if it equals one of
the ti . Thus, one can decide if υ belongs to T, so T is recursive. If, additionally, A is
bounded, then B is finite and hence recursive. Thus, A is a recursive system.           u

      It seems plausible that any realistic accounting system will have a finite trans-
action base. An example of a specific allowable transaction might be a mortgage or
other fixed payment. On the other hand, a debit to cash and a credit to accounts
receivable is an example of an allowable transaction type in which any amounts are
permitted. This allowable type would have a “ +” in cash and a “ –” in accounts
receivable and “0”s elsewhere. For a bounded accounting system, the allowable trans-
actions can be represented as either specific transactions and allowable types with
associated bounds or, equivalently, as a listing of specific transactions only. This is
true since the finite sets of bounded allowable types can be listed as finite sequences
of specific transactions and the finite union of finite sets remains a finite set. This
representation would therefore remain a recursive system.
