7.1. Finitely specified accounting systems
      In addition to having a finite transaction base, a realistic accounting system will
most likely have finite upper and lower bounds, mi and Mi , for the balance of its ith
account for all accounts of the system. These bounds can be thought of as n × 1
column vectors m and M, where mi ≤ Mi . A realistic system will also have certain al-
lowable quotient systems corresponding to reports that the system could reasonably
be required to generate. Thus, an n-account system will have a list of allowable
equivalence relations on the set {1, 2,…, n}. These considerations suggest the follow-
ing type of accounting system as being the closest to a realistic system of the
systems considered here.
      An accounting system A on n accounts is said to be finitely specified if it has
a finite transaction base, is bounded and is associated with a set of equivalence rela-
tions on the set of accounts. An appropriate notation for A is the sextuple

                                 A = (A|T0|T1|m|M|E),
196            R.A. Nehmer, D. Robinson y The representation of accounting systems



where A is the set of accounts, T0 is a finite set of specific allowable transactions, T1
is a set of allowable transaction types, m and M are the lower and upper bound vectors,
and E is a set of equivalence relations on {1, 2,…, n}. An attractive feature of such a
finitely specified system is the obvious possibility of machine implementation.
      In order to specify A for the purpose of machine implementation, one must
specify:
(i)   a finite subset T0 of Bal(n);
(ii) a finite set T1 of n × 1 column vectors with entries –1, 0, +1 corresponding to
     type vectors with entries –, 0, +;
(iii) two n × 1 column vectors m and M over Z such that mi ≤ Mi ;
(iv) a finite list E of equivalence relations on {1, 2,…, n} described by either parti-
     tions or n-square boolean matrices.
     In principle, it is a straightforward matter to write a piece of computer code
which will incorporate the above data and the rules for forming new balances, and so
can operate the accounting system A .

8.    Verification of balances

      Consider an accounting system A = (A|T|B) on n accounts. Suppose that b(0)
is the balance vector of A at some point of time. One can ask of an arbitrary vector
b in Bal(n) whether it could be the balance vector of A at some future time. One
question is thus whether b could be obtained by applying a finite sequence of trans-
actions in T to b(0). One can further inquire if there is such a sequence which produces
intermediate balance vectors that all belong to B. Accounting systems for which there
are procedures to answer these questions will have a capability to verify final balances
at the end of an accounting period. Systems with a finite transaction base are of this
type, as the following result shows.

