9.   Conclusions
     This work has described a useful algebraic structure for the representation of
accounting systems. The use of vectors to contain the accounting data for transactions
198             R.A. Nehmer, D. Robinson y The representation of accounting systems



and balances in the system is the starting point of this new structure. This allows the
system to be abstractly conceived of as a group or semi-group and also induces the
monoid space within which the system operates. The introduction of the concept of
partitioning the accounts and providing homomorphisms to control the generation of
reports gives the system an ability to represent most of the manipulations which occur
in realistic accounting systems. In addition, providing a description of the accounting
system in terms of abstract algebra provides convenient parallels into the theories of
Turing machines and computability.
      Future research by the authors is proceeding in three general directions. The first
is to add elements to the description which will adequately provide for concepts of
managerial control to be given through the algebraic structure. This will bring the
abstract structure closer to real-world accounting systems. The second area of con-
tinuing research is a graph theoretic description of the accounting system as a series
of flows to and from the different accounts. This research promises to provide some
interesting insights into the problems of storage of accounting data in computerized
accounting systems. The final research direction involves methods to be used in the
comparison of accounting systems which are represented in the manner described in
this paper. These comparisons will provide a richer structure for the comparison of
information systems generally, as Nehmer [3] shows.

